#!/bin/sh
# script/ci-build: every check that gates a pull request, in ONE enumeration.
#
# **Why this file is shaped like a table** (milestone 286). Until 2026-09-13 the set of gating
# checks was written down twice: this script plus `script/gates` held the list a developer runs, and
# `.github/workflows/ci.yml` held its own list as parallel jobs. Nothing compared them, and the
# evidence that this was rung four rather than a design is that the divergence had already happened
# three times in prose nobody re-derived:
#
#   * `ci.yml` said of `script/icount` "it is not in script/test and it is not in script/gates".
#     It had been in `script/gates` since milestone 62.
#   * `notes/scripts.md` listed `gates` as "`script/fmt --check`, `script/lint`, `script/test`,
#     then `script/test --hvf`", three milestones after `image-permissions`, `icount` and
#     `swish-check` joined it.
#   * `CONTRIBUTING.md` and `.github/pull_request_template.md` both said "the five checks". There
#     were six, and `script/gates` itself printed six names on every run.
#
# Each of those was correct when written. The ladder in `AGENTS.md` says to move up a rung when a
# fact exists only as a comment, and the rung above a gate that compares two lists is **one list**.
# So the table below is the only place the set lives: CI fans it out by name, a developer runs it
# with no arguments, and a seventh check cannot be added to one without the other learning about it.
#
# **The two tiers, and the principle that forced them.** It cannot be that "no arguments" means
# everything: `script/verify` is about 47 minutes of Kani and `script/cpu-matrix` is five QEMU
# boots, and `script/gates`' own header carried the rule that has to survive this milestone, which
# is that **a gate people skip is not a gate**. So the enumeration carries a tier column and the
# two tiers are two tags in one table rather than two files:
#
#   * `local`  a developer waits for it before pushing, and the no-argument path runs it.
#   * `ci`     only a runner waits for it. Nameable here, never run by the no-argument path.
#
# **The tier is data; the reason a check is `ci` is still prose**, and that half cannot be
# mechanised: "it builds the kernel test binary twice" is a judgement about what a person will wait
# for. What changed is that the prose now sits in the same table as the tag it explains, so a
# comment can no longer claim a membership the code contradicts. Each `ci` row below says why.
#
# **The tier tags and the no-argument meaning are PROVISIONAL** pending calef:
# milestone 440 (what `script/ci-build` with no arguments should mean),
# `design/roadmap/0440-what-no-arguments-means.md`, states the options and their costs.
#
# **Order is cheapest first, and it is the table's order.** That is the whole value of the
# no-argument path to a developer: a formatting slip costs twenty seconds rather than ten minutes.
# Do not reorder these rows for tidiness.
#
# **Bootstrapping is the no-argument path's job and nobody else's, and it runs FIRST rather than
# lazily.** calef ruled this on 2026-09-13 and it is the shipped behaviour: a machine that cannot
# provision will fail the later rows anyway, and failing early is honest. It is a real cost against
# the cheapest-first rule and it is priced rather than hidden, since a formatting slip now costs
# `script/bootstrap` plus twenty seconds. Lazy provisioning would buy almost nothing, because `lint`
# is the second row and needs `shellcheck`, `cargo-machete` and `typos`, all of which bootstrap
# installs; so the saving would be one row wide and the cost would be a second column in the table
# saying which rows need it.
#
# **What that ruling costs, and why the failure path below is loud.** `script/bootstrap` does two
# jobs: it installs what is missing, and it verifies the environment is ADEQUATE. The second can
# fail on a machine where most of this tier would have run perfectly; measured on milestone 286's
# own container, where the packaged QEMU is 8.2.2 and lacks `riscv-iommu-pci`, so bootstrap exits 1
# having installed nothing and broken nothing. The developer then gets **one error about QEMU** and
# no checks at all, where the retired `script/gates` (which never provisioned) ran three. Read as
# the QEMU error alone, that is a gate silently giving somebody nothing, which is the same failure
# `a gate people skip is not a gate` names, arriving from the other side. So the exit says in plain
# words that NO CHECKS RAN, lists the tier it skipped out of this table rather than out of a second
# hand-written list, and names what is still runnable by hand.
# **Bootstrapping is the no-argument path's job and nobody else's.** With no arguments this is the
# one command a person runs before pushing, so it provisions first; `script/bootstrap` is idempotent
# and does nothing on a warm machine. A named check runs exactly what it names, because the caller
# that names one check is a CI job whose own steps have already installed the targets, the
# components and the QEMU it needs. That is not a new convention: the `bench` and `cpu matrix` jobs
# in `ci.yml` have always carried their own `script/bootstrap` step, and the `test` job now does
# too.
#
# Usage:
#   script/ci-build                 the `local` tier, in this table's order, cheapest first
#   script/ci-build <check>...      exactly those checks, in the order given
#   script/ci-build --list          print the table
#
# This script never writes. `script/fmt` with no argument is the one that formats.
#
# See notes/scripts.md, notes/check-inventory.md and .github/workflows/ci.yml.
#
# Name: ratified 2026-07-22 by adopting Scripts to Rule Them All. The canonical set keeps its
# standard names even where a more descriptive word exists, because the entire value is that the
# command is the same in every repository that follows the pattern (design/naming.md). Respelled from
# `cibuild` on 2026-08-01 (milestone 63) under the rule that shell entry points are hyphenated,
# because an underscore or a squash in a command name reads as a mistake next to `apt-get` and
# `pkg-config`. Milestone 286 widened what it does without touching the name, and the widening is
# what the name always promised: "build it the way CI does" is exactly a set of checks a developer
# can run. It absorbed `script/gates`, which was unratified, was `DECISIONS §134`'s one clear
# exception (a noun that acts), and is retired rather than kept as a wrapper, because two names for
# one thing is the comprehension cost the naming rules exist to prevent.

set -e
# A heavy job: wait for one of the machine's heavy-job slots first (helpers/heavy_slot.py).
. "$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/heavy-slot.sh"
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

# This project's own QEMU, if `script/ci-qemu` has built one (helpers/qemu-path.sh says why
# PATH is the mechanism). A no-op on macOS and on any machine without that prefix.
. helpers/qemu-path.sh

# --- the enumeration --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `name|tier|command`. One row per check. The command is evaluated by `sh`, so a row may chain.
#
# The `swish-check` row chains `cargo xtask std-exerciser` because the check's boots carry the
# exerciser's ELF only when it is built (xtask/src/swish_check/boot.rs says why), and the monolithic
# build+test job used to get it from `test` running first in the same runner. Split into one job
# per check (2026-09-30), that ordering died and CI refused to skip the std line, correctly. The
# dependency lives in the row now, where CI and a developer both meet it, instead of in a job's
# position in a chain.
#
# **The same rows fetch `rg` from basalt first** (milestone 810 (`ripgrep` is packaged in basalt and
# installed with `jig`)), for the same reason: the check types `rg`'s lines, including milestone 121
# (`ripgrep` on nife)'s walk of a copy of `crates/`, and `jig install ripgrep`, only when `cargo
# xtask basalt-fetch` has fetched basalt's published package, and in CI it refuses to skip them.
# The fetch is basalt's Release asset for each architecture, accepted only under the SHA-256
# basalt's recipe records at the commit `xtask/basalt-pin` names, and ripgrep's own host build for the reference answer, pinned the
# same way. calef, 2026-10-13 (UTC): "1 is preferable because that tests jig and basalt's delivery
# mechanism as well."
#
# **CI runs `swish-check` as the two `ci` rows after it** (milestone 628 (provisional); the row
# names are provisional): the aarch64 and riscv64 legs on the arm64 runner, whose weakly ordered
# host is why they stay there, and the x86_64 leg on an x86_64 runner, where it boots under KVM.
# Together they are exactly the `local` row, which is what a developer runs. ci.yml's
# `swish-check-x86_64` job has the measurement. The `local` row and the aarch64 row end with the
# `--input-race` boot, the input driver's red test (notes/swish-check-flake-2026w42.md). It comes
# last because its program feature rebuilds `components`, which the plain legs must not boot.
#
# **CI runs `test` as the two `ci` rows after it** (lane maintainer/split-test-job, 2026-10-12 UTC,
# #1930; the row names are provisional): the host pass with the aarch64 and riscv64 legs, and the
# x86_64 leg alone with `--no-host`, because the first row ran the host pass on the same commit.
# Together they are exactly the `local` row. ci.yml's `test-shard` job has the measurement.
#
# Adding a check here is the whole of adding a check: the no-argument path picks it up if the tier
# is `local`, `--list` prints it, and `ci.yml` names it in a job. Adding it to `ci.yml` WITHOUT a row
# here is the defect this table exists to prevent.
checks() {
    cat <<'TABLE'
fmt|local|script/fmt --check
lint|local|script/lint
image-permissions|local|script/image-permissions
icount|local|script/icount
build-is-reproducible|local|script/build-is-reproducible
test|local|script/test
test-aarch64-riscv64|ci|script/test --arch aarch64,riscv64
test-x86_64|ci|script/test --arch x86_64 --no-host
swish-check|local|cargo xtask std-exerciser && cargo xtask basalt-fetch && script/swish-check && script/swish-check --arch aarch64 --input-race
swish-check-aarch64-riscv64|ci|cargo xtask std-exerciser && cargo xtask basalt-fetch && script/swish-check --arch aarch64 && script/swish-check --arch riscv64 && script/swish-check --arch aarch64 --input-race
swish-check-x86_64|ci|cargo xtask std-exerciser && cargo xtask basalt-fetch && script/swish-check --arch x86_64
rg-threads-aarch64-riscv64|ci|cargo xtask basalt-fetch && NIFE_RIPGREP_REQUIRED=1 cargo test -p walk_pricing && NIFE_RIPGREP_REQUIRED=1 NIFE_SMP=4 script/test --arch aarch64,riscv64 --test ripgrep && NIFE_IOPING_REQUIRED=1 NIFE_HACKBENCH_REQUIRED=1 NIFE_SPEEDTEST1_REQUIRED=1 script/test --arch aarch64,riscv64 --test unmodified_
rg-threads-x86_64|ci|cargo xtask basalt-fetch && NIFE_RIPGREP_REQUIRED=1 NIFE_SMP=4 script/test --arch x86_64 --test ripgrep && NIFE_IOPING_REQUIRED=1 NIFE_HACKBENCH_REQUIRED=1 NIFE_SPEEDTEST1_REQUIRED=1 script/test --arch x86_64 --test unmodified_
boot-check|local|script/boot-check
release-uefi-boot|ci|cargo xtask uefi-boot --release
acpi-boot|ci|cargo xtask acpi-boot
netboot-rehearsal|ci|cargo xtask uefi-image --release && script/netboot-rehearsal --check && script/netboot-rehearsal && script/netboot-rehearsal --mac 02:00:00:00:00:01
hvf|local|hvf_leg
fastpath-footprint|ci|script/fastpath-footprint
boot-file-size-check|ci|script/boot-file-size-check
stack-frame-check|ci|script/stack-frame-check
stack-depth-check|ci|script/stack-depth-check
supply-chain|ci|script/supply-chain
coverage|ci|script/coverage
fuzz|ci|script/fuzz --time 60
bench|ci|script/bench --check && script/bench --riscv --check && script/bench --x86 --check
cpu-matrix|ci|script/cpu-matrix
watchdog-check|ci|script/soak-test --wedge --arch x86_64 && script/soak-test --watchdog --arch x86_64
TABLE
}

# Why each `ci` row is not `local`. Kept beside the tier it explains, because the three comments
# this milestone deleted from `ci.yml` were each correct when written and each drifted.
#
#   test-aarch64-riscv64 and test-x86_64  the `test` row cut in two for CI's 20-minute job budget;
#                       a developer runs the whole row.
#   rg-threads-aarch64-riscv64 and rg-threads-x86_64  `rg` walking in parallel at four cores
#                       (milestone 121, fatal risk 1's third gap; lane milestone/121-rg-threads,
#                       2026-10-12 UTC). They rebuild every leg's test image with a filter and boot
#                       it at `NIFE_SMP=4`, which nothing else boots for the suite, so there is
#                       nothing warm to share. A developer runs them by name. `NIFE_RIPGREP_REQUIRED`
#                       turns a missing `rg` from a skip into a failure, here and in `walk_pricing`'s
#                       host check, which only the aarch64 row runs. The same rows then boot the
#                       suite filtered to `unmodified_`, at one core, because they have already
#                       fetched basalt's packages: ioping (milestone 906 (ioping is packaged in
#                       basalt)), hackbench (milestone 907 (hackbench is packaged in basalt)) and
#                       SQLite's speedtest1 (milestone 897 (SQLite is packaged in basalt)), and
#                       `rg`'s one-core test comes along. Each `NIFE_<PROGRAM>_REQUIRED` refuses
#                       its program's skip. An exception to the rows' names, kept because a row of
#                       its own would provision a runner for it.
#   fastpath-footprint  builds two RELEASE kernels, which no other check builds, so there is
#                       nothing warm to share and a developer would wait for a cold build.
#   release-uefi-boot   boots the release x86_64 UEFI image under OVMF to the shell, the profile
#                       every image for a machine is built in (`install-boot`, `network-bench`).
#                       A debug boot cannot stand in: an `asm!` operand that shares a register
#                       with a live input is harmless at debug and fatal at release. It builds a
#                       release kernel and archive that no `local` row builds, so CI runs it in the
#                       `boot-check` job after that row instead of a developer before each push.
#   acpi-boot           boots the release aarch64 stick under EDK2 with `acpi=on`, the description
#                       every aarch64 cloud machine gives (milestone 578 (an ACPI discovery path
#                       for aarch64)). It builds a release
#                       aarch64 kernel and archive that no `local` row builds, so it rides in the
#                       `boot-check` job beside `release-uefi-boot` for the same reason.
#   netboot-rehearsal   PXE-boots the release x86_64 UEFI image under OVMF through the real
#                       `script/board-netboot`, with DHCP answered out of
#                       `bench/xenon-netboot/dnsmasq.conf`, then checks that a MAC the config does
#                       not name gets no boot file (milestone 260 (boot xenon over the network)).
#                       It reuses the release kernel `release-uefi-boot` builds, so it rides in the
#                       `boot-check` job after that row.
#   boot-file-size-check builds the release x86_64 UEFI image (a release kernel and archive, which
#                       only `stick` and `install-boot` build otherwise) to budget its size.
#   stack-frame-check   builds the kernel test binary twice; `-Z emit-stack-sizes` changes the
#                       build, so the artifacts the `test` check just made cannot be reused.
#   stack-depth-check   the same build as the row above, sharing its artifacts.
#   supply-chain        advisories and licences are a property of the dependency graph on the day
#                       it is audited, not of the diff; `cargo-deny` also wants the network.
#   coverage            instruments and re-runs the whole host suite.
#   fuzz                60 seconds per target by construction, and unbounded by intent.
#   bench               three full icount baselines; `script/icount` above is the seven-second
#                       instrument that carries the timing claims, and it IS local.
#   cpu-matrix          five QEMU boots of the riscv64 suite, one per CPU model.
#   watchdog-check      two x86_64 soaks against the wall clock, about six minutes, and most of it
#                       waiting: a watchdog is proven by letting time pass (milestone 593 (a wedged
#                       kernel resets itself)).
#
# `script/verify` (Kani, ~47 minutes) and the re-falsification sweep are deliberately absent: they
# are `verify.yml`, a different workflow with its own sharding, and this table's claim is about the
# checks `ci.yml` runs. Naming them here without running them would be a fourth kind of prose that
# nothing keeps true. See notes/check-inventory.md for the whole surface.

# The silicon leg (milestone 81), a check rather than a line because everything else here is a row.
#
# Everything above it runs under TCG, which emulates every instruction: a machine with no real
# caches, no real reordering, and a scheduler QEMU round-robins between vCPUs. Hypervisor.framework
# runs the same kernel at guest EL1 on the physical Apple Silicon core, four vCPUs on four host
# threads at once, and that is a genuinely different machine to be correct on. It found a scheduler
# test asserting against a yield count on its first run.
#
# It is `local` and has no CI job because GitHub's hosted macOS arm64 runners are themselves virtual
# machines with no nested virtualization, so HVF does not exist there. This is the one command a
# person runs before pushing, so this is where the leg reaches a real core.
#
# aarch64 only, and that is the honest name for it: HVF runs the host's own ISA, and riscv64 has no
# equivalent until the board lands.
hvf_missing=""
hvf_leg() {
    if [ "$(uname -s)" != "Darwin" ]; then
        hvf_missing="this host is not macOS"
    elif [ "$(uname -m)" != "arm64" ]; then
        hvf_missing="this host is not Apple Silicon"
    elif [ "$(sysctl -n kern.hv_support 2>/dev/null)" != "1" ]; then
        hvf_missing="Hypervisor.framework is unavailable (kern.hv_support is not 1; a VM, or entitlements)"
    elif ! qemu-system-aarch64 -accel help 2>/dev/null | grep -q '^hvf$'; then
        hvf_missing="the QEMU on PATH was built without the hvf accelerator"
    else
        # The four conditions above ask whether HVF exists. This one asks whether QEMU will start the
        # machine the runner actually configures, which is a different question and the one that was
        # being answered by a crash (milestone 222). On QEMU 11.1.1 HVF refuses GICv2; the kernel has
        # driven GICv3 too since milestone 227 and the runner asks for it under HVF, so today the probe
        # passes. It stays because the answer is QEMU's to change. Asking the runner rather than
        # testing a QEMU version keeps the machine string in one place; see the probe in
        # helpers/qemu-runner-aarch64.sh.
        if ! hvf_refusal="$(NIFE_PROBE=1 NIFE_ACCEL=hvf helpers/qemu-runner-aarch64.sh 2>/dev/null)"; then
            hvf_missing="QEMU will not start the machine this leg needs. It said: $hvf_refusal. The kernel drives GICv2 and GICv3 and the runner asks HVF for a GICv3, so this refusal is about something else; notes/hvf-leg.md has the history"
        fi
    fi

    # **Skip LOUDLY.** A silent omission would let a transcript from a Linux CI runner read exactly
    # like one from this laptop, and the whole point of the leg is that those two are not the same
    # evidence. One line, naming what did not run and why, so nobody has to infer it from an absence.
    if [ -n "$hvf_missing" ]; then
        echo "ci-build: SKIPPED the HVF leg ($hvf_missing). NO code in this run executed on a physical core; everything else in this run was TCG emulation."
    else
        # It named one known HVF-only flake here from milestone 227 (a GICv3 driver, because GICv2
        # boots and silently loses every interrupt) until milestone 514 (the HVF std listener hangs
        # in some full runs) closed it on 2026-10-12. A red here is now yours until shown
        # otherwise.
        if ! script/test --hvf; then
            echo "ci-build: the HVF leg failed; no HVF-only failure is known."
            return 1
        fi
    fi
}

# --- the machinery ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

command_for() {
    checks | awk -F'|' -v want="$1" '$1 == want { print $3; found = 1 } END { exit !found }'
}

names_in_tier() {
    checks | awk -F'|' -v want="$1" '$2 == want { print $1 }'
}

usage() {
    echo "usage: script/ci-build [--list | <check>...]" >&2
    echo "  no arguments runs the 'local' tier, cheapest first:" >&2
    echo "    $(names_in_tier local | tr '\n' ' ')" >&2
    echo "  CI-only checks, runnable by name:" >&2
    echo "    $(names_in_tier ci | tr '\n' ' ')" >&2
    echo "  To FORMAT rather than check, run script/fmt. This script never writes." >&2
}

run_one() {
    echo
    echo "==> script/ci-build $1"
    # One UTC line at each boundary, calef 2026-09-30, after a build+test step sat at 33+ minutes
    # with every other job of the run done at 13: the split-or-not decision is being made from a
    # week of these lines rather than one night's impression. Nothing parses them; they are for the
    # person reading a job log. `date -u` because every date in this tree is UTC.
    echo "ci-build: $1 begins $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
    cmd="$(command_for "$1")" || { echo "script/ci-build: no such check '$1'" >&2; usage; exit 2; }
    if eval "$cmd"; then
        echo "ci-build: $1 ends $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ), exit 0"
    else
        status=$?
        echo "ci-build: $1 ends $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ), exit $status"
        exit $status
    fi
}

case "$1" in
--list)
    [ $# -eq 1 ] || { usage; exit 2; }
    printf '%-22s %-6s %s\n' check tier runs
    checks | awk -F'|' '{ printf "%-22s %-6s %s\n", $1, $2, $3 }'
    exit 0
    ;;
-h | --help)
    usage
    exit 0
    ;;
-*)
    echo "script/ci-build: unknown option '$1'" >&2
    usage
    exit 2
    ;;
esac

if [ $# -gt 0 ]; then
    # Named checks. Validate the whole list BEFORE running any of it, so a typo in the last name
    # does not surface after the first check has spent ten minutes.
    for name in "$@"; do
        command_for "$name" >/dev/null || {
            echo "script/ci-build: no such check '$name'" >&2
            usage
            exit 2
        }
    done
    for name in "$@"; do
        run_one "$name"
    done
    echo
    if [ -n "$hvf_missing" ]; then
        echo "ci-build: $* all pass (TCG only; the HVF leg was skipped)"
    else
        echo "ci-build: $* all pass"
    fi
    exit 0
fi

# The no-argument path: provision, then the `local` tier in this table's order.
#
# **A failed bootstrap ends the run, loudly, saying what it cost.** `set -e` would already exit here
# and would leave the developer holding whatever sentence `script/bootstrap` printed last, which on
# the machine this was measured on is a line about a QEMU device. The point of the block below is
# that nobody should have to infer "and therefore nothing was checked" from the absence of output.
echo "==> script/bootstrap"
if ! script/bootstrap; then
    echo
    echo "ci-build: NO CHECKS RAN. script/bootstrap failed above, and every check below it needs"
    echo "  something it provisions: the toolchain components, the three emulators, or the lint tools."
    echo "  Read its last line as the CAUSE, not as the whole cost. The cost is this:"
    echo
    echo "  Skipped, the entire local tier:"
    names_in_tier local | sed 's/^/    /'
    echo
    echo "  Still runnable by name on a machine in this state:"
    echo "    script/ci-build fmt      rustfmt only; needs nothing script/bootstrap installs"
    echo
    # **`fmt` is named by hand and the others are not, and that is deliberate.** Which rows survive a
    # failed bootstrap depends on WHICH PART of it failed, and this table has no column for that: an
    # adequacy failure (an old QEMU) leaves `lint` and `image-permissions` perfectly runnable, while a
    # missing rustup leaves nothing. Guessing from the table would produce a list that is wrong half
    # the time, which is worse than a short list that is always right. `fmt` is the one row that is
    # safe under every failure mode, so it is the one named.
    echo "  Other rows may also work, depending on what failed: an out-of-date emulator leaves"
    echo "  script/ci-build lint and script/ci-build image-permissions runnable, where a missing"
    echo "  rustup leaves nothing. This script does not guess which, because the table does not"
    echo "  record why a row needs bootstrap and a wrong guess is worse than a short list."
    exit 1
fi

# The tier has to select something: milestone 401 (a gate that selects the set it judges).
# `names_in_tier` matches a literal column value out of the `checks` table, so renaming the tier or reformatting the separator leaves this
# loop nothing to iterate, and the script then prints "all pass" having run no check at all. An
# empty selection is the one outcome that must never read as success.
if [ -z "$(names_in_tier local)" ]; then
    echo "ci-build: the 'local' tier selected no checks, so nothing ran. The tier column in" >&2
    echo "checks() moved; without this guard the run would have reported 'all pass'." >&2
    exit 1
fi

ran=""
for name in $(names_in_tier local); do
    run_one "$name"
    ran="$ran $name"
done

echo
if [ -n "$hvf_missing" ]; then
    echo "ci-build:$ran all pass (TCG only; the HVF leg was skipped)"
else
    echo "ci-build:$ran all pass, the aarch64 suite twice (TCG and the physical core)"
fi
